  "stage1Description": "An X-Ray CAT scan (Computed Axial Tomography) non-invasively reconstructs the 2D interior of an object by transmitting X-ray beams through it from all angles θ. Detectors measure transmitted intensity, which by the Beer-Lambert law yields the mathematical line integral of the absorption coefficient f(x, y) along each line L: g(L) = ∫_L f ds. The core tomography challenge is inverting these line integrals to recover the interior.",
  "@stage1Description": {
    "description": "Full description text for Stage 1 in wide layout"
  },
  "stage1Instruction1": "Click or drag in Object Space to see all lines passing through that point. Click \"Next\" or Stage 2 above to continue.",
  "@stage1Instruction1": {
    "description": "Interaction prompt for Stage 1 in wide layout"
  },
  "stage1NarrowDescription": "A CAT scan reconstructs the 2D interior of an object non-invasively by transmitting X-rays through it at many angles θ. Detectors measure absorbed intensity, yielding the line integral of absorption f(x, y) along each line L: g(L) = ∫_L f ds.",
  "@stage1NarrowDescription": {
    "description": "Concise description text for Stage 1 in narrow layout"
  },
  "stage1NarrowInstruction1": "Tap or drag in Object Space to see lines through that point. Tap \"Next\" or Stage 2 above to continue.",
  "@stage1NarrowInstruction1": {
    "description": "Interaction prompt for Stage 1 in narrow layout"
  },
  "stage1NarrowSubtitle": "Overview & Radon Transform",
  "@stage1NarrowSubtitle": {
    "description": "Subtitle for Stage 1 in narrow layout"
  },
  "stage1NarrowTitle": "Tomography Overview",
  "@stage1NarrowTitle": {
    "description": "Short title for Stage 1 in narrow layout"
  },
  "stage1Subtitle": "Overview & The Radon Transform",
  "@stage1Subtitle": {
    "description": "Subtitle for Stage 1 in wide layout"
  },
  "stage1Title": "Computed Axial Tomography",
  "@stage1Title": {
    "description": "Title for Stage 1 in wide layout"
  },
  "stage2Description": "Every straight line in 2D space is parameterized by two variables: its signed perpendicular distance r from the origin, and its normal angle θ: x·cos(θ) + y·sin(θ) = r. The left viewport displays physical Object Space (x, y), and the right viewport displays Sinogram Space (r, θ). Each individual point (r, θ) in sinogram space represents a complete straight X-ray line traversing object space.",
  "@stage2Description": {
    "description": "Full description text for Stage 2 in wide layout"
  },
  "stage2Instruction1": "Step 1 of 3: Click or drag anywhere in the Sinogram on the right to see the corresponding line x·cos(θ) + y·sin(θ) = r and normal on the left.",
  "@stage2Instruction1": {
    "description": "Step 1 interaction prompt for Stage 2 in wide layout"
  },
  "stage2Instruction2": "Step 2 of 3: Drag left and right in the Sinogram to vary radial distance r (-1 to 1). Notice how the line translates in Object Space.",
  "@stage2Instruction2": {
    "description": "Step 2 interaction prompt for Stage 2 in wide layout"
  },
  "stage2Instruction3": "Step 3 of 3: Drag up and down in the Sinogram to vary angle θ (0 to 2π). Notice how the line rotates in Object Space.",
  "@stage2Instruction3": {
    "description": "Step 3 interaction prompt for Stage 2 in wide layout"
  },
  "stage2NarrowDescription": "Every straight line in the plane is parameterized by its signed perpendicular distance r from the origin and normal angle θ: x·cos(θ) + y·sin(θ) = r. A single point (r, θ) in the bottom canvas represents a complete line traversing object space on the top.",
  "@stage2NarrowDescription": {
    "description": "Concise description text for Stage 2 in narrow layout"
  },
  "stage2NarrowInstruction1": "Step 1 of 3: Click or drag in the Sinogram on the bottom to see the corresponding line and normal on the top.",
  "@stage2NarrowInstruction1": {
    "description": "Step 1 interaction prompt for Stage 2 in narrow layout"
  },
  "stage2NarrowInstruction2": "Step 2 of 3: Drag left and right in the Sinogram to vary radial distance r (-1 to 1). Notice the line translation.",
  "@stage2NarrowInstruction2": {
    "description": "Step 2 interaction prompt for Stage 2 in narrow layout"
  },
  "stage2NarrowInstruction3": "Step 3 of 3: Drag up and down in the Sinogram to vary angle θ (0 to 2π). Notice the line rotation.",
  "@stage2NarrowInstruction3": {
    "description": "Step 3 interaction prompt for Stage 2 in narrow layout"
  },
  "stage2NarrowSubtitle": "Line Equation: x·cos(θ) + y·sin(θ) = r",
  "@stage2NarrowSubtitle": {
    "description": "Subtitle for Stage 2 in narrow layout"
  },
  "stage2NarrowTitle": "Object on Top, Data on Bottom",
  "@stage2NarrowTitle": {
    "description": "Short title for Stage 2 in narrow layout"
  },
  "stage2Subtitle": "Line Parameterization: x·cos(θ) + y·sin(θ) = r",
  "@stage2Subtitle": {
    "description": "Subtitle for Stage 2 in wide layout"
  },
  "stage2Title": "Object on Left, Data on Right",
  "@stage2Title": {
    "description": "Title for Stage 2 in wide layout"
  },
  "stage3Description": "All lines passing through a single fixed point (x, y) satisfy r(θ) = x·cos(θ) + y·sin(θ). As the projection angle θ varies from 0 to 2π, this family of lines forms an exact sinusoidal wave in (r, θ) sinogram space. This duality is fundamental: a point in object space maps to a sinusoid in sinogram space, while a point in sinogram space maps to a line in object space. Clicking the border bars shows lines with constant θ or constant r.",
  "@stage3Description": {
    "description": "Full description text for Stage 3 in wide layout"
  },
  "stage3Instruction1": "Step 1 of 3: Click or drag in Object Space on the left to move the probe point and watch its sinusoidal dual curve on the right.",
  "@stage3Instruction1": {
    "description": "Step 1 interaction prompt for Stage 3 in wide layout"
  },
  "stage3Instruction2": "Step 2 of 3: Click the right border bar in the Sinogram to display a family of parallel lines across all r at fixed angle θ.",
  "@stage3Instruction2": {
    "description": "Step 2 interaction prompt for Stage 3 in wide layout"
  },
  "stage3Instruction3": "Step 3 of 3: Click the bottom border bar in the Sinogram to view lines tangent to a concentric circle of radius |r|.",
  "@stage3Instruction3": {
    "description": "Step 3 interaction prompt for Stage 3 in wide layout"
  },
  "stage3NarrowDescription": "Every line passing through a fixed point (x, y) satisfies r(θ) = x·cos(θ) + y·sin(θ). As θ varies from 0 to 2π, this family of lines forms an exact sinusoidal wave in (r, θ) space.",
  "@stage3NarrowDescription": {
    "description": "Concise description text for Stage 3 in narrow layout"
  },
  "stage3NarrowInstruction1": "Step 1 of 3: Click or drag in Object Space on the top to move the probe point and watch its sinusoidal dual curve on the bottom.",
  "@stage3NarrowInstruction1": {
    "description": "Step 1 interaction prompt for Stage 3 in narrow layout"
  },
  "stage3NarrowInstruction2": "Step 2 of 3: Click the right border bar in the Sinogram to display a family of parallel lines at fixed angle θ.",
  "@stage3NarrowInstruction2": {
    "description": "Step 2 interaction prompt for Stage 3 in narrow layout"
  },
  "stage3NarrowInstruction3": "Step 3 of 3: Click the bottom border bar in the Sinogram to view lines tangent to a circle of radius |r|.",
  "@stage3NarrowInstruction3": {
    "description": "Step 3 interaction prompt for Stage 3 in narrow layout"
  },
  "stage3NarrowSubtitle": "Points Trace Sinusoids in Sinogram",
  "@stage3NarrowSubtitle": {
    "description": "Subtitle for Stage 3 in narrow layout"
  },
  "stage3Subtitle": "Points in Object Space Trace Sinusoids in Sinogram Space",
  "@stage3Subtitle": {
    "description": "Subtitle for Stage 3 in wide layout"
  },
  "stage3Title": "Point Duality",
  "@stage3Title": {
    "description": "Title for Stage 3 in wide and narrow layouts"
  },
  "stage4Description": "A phantom object—a circular disk simulating interior structure—is placed in object space. Its X-ray attenuation creates a 2D shadow dataset g(r, θ) called a sinogram. There is faint intensity when an X-ray only skims the disk edge, and strong intensity when it passes through the diameter. Dragging the phantom shifts the amplitude and phase of its sinusoidal shadow band.",
  "@stage4Description": {
    "description": "Full description text for Stage 4 in wide layout"
  },
  "stage4Instruction1": "Step 1 of 2: Drag the red phantom disk in Object Space on the left to reposition it.",
  "@stage4Instruction1": {
    "description": "Step 1 interaction prompt for Stage 4 in wide layout"
  },
  "stage4Instruction2": "Step 2 of 2: Observe how the phantom’s 2D sinogram shadow immediately shifts on the right, encoding its location.",
  "@stage4Instruction2": {
    "description": "Step 2 interaction prompt for Stage 4 in wide layout"
  },
  "stage4NarrowDescription": "A phantom object (circular disk) is placed in object space. Its X-ray projections form a 2D shadow dataset g(r, θ) known as a sinogram. Dragging the phantom shifts its shadow band.",
  "@stage4NarrowDescription": {
    "description": "Concise description text for Stage 4 in narrow layout"
  },
  "stage4NarrowInstruction1": "Step 1 of 2: Drag the red phantom disk in Object Space on the top to reposition it.",
  "@stage4NarrowInstruction1": {
    "description": "Step 1 interaction prompt for Stage 4 in narrow layout"
  },
  "stage4NarrowInstruction2": "Step 2 of 2: Observe how the phantom’s sinogram shadow shifts on the bottom as you drag.",
  "@stage4NarrowInstruction2": {
    "description": "Step 2 interaction prompt for Stage 4 in narrow layout"
  },
  "stage4NarrowSubtitle": "Phantom & 2D Sinogram Shadow",
  "@stage4NarrowSubtitle": {
    "description": "Subtitle for Stage 4 in narrow layout"
  },
  "stage4NarrowTitle": "Object Shadow",
  "@stage4NarrowTitle": {
    "description": "Short title for Stage 4 in narrow layout"
  },
  "stage4Subtitle": "The Phantom Object & Its 2D Sinogram Shadow",
  "@stage4Subtitle": {
    "description": "Subtitle for Stage 4 in wide layout"
  },
  "stage4Title": "Object Produces Shadow",
  "@stage4Title": {
    "description": "Title for Stage 4 in wide layout"
  },
  "stage5Description": "The bottom graph displays 1D cross-sections of the sinogram g(r, θ). In particular, examining projections along the sinusoidal curve of a point (x, y) and summing them over all angles θ is called backprojection: ∫₀²π g(x·cos θ + y·sin θ, θ) dθ. If the point lies inside the phantom disk, the integral is large; if outside, it is small. However, unfiltered backprojection blurs features by a 1/|r| point-spread function.",
  "@stage5Description": {
    "description": "Full description text for Stage 5 in wide layout"
  },
  "stage5Instruction1": "Step 1 of 3: Click inside the phantom disk on the left. Notice the large backprojection integral value on the bottom graph.",
  "@stage5Instruction1": {
    "description": "Step 1 interaction prompt for Stage 5 in wide layout"
  },
  "stage5Instruction2": "Step 2 of 3: Click outside the phantom disk. Observe how the 1D profile values drop and the backprojection integral becomes small.",
  "@stage5Instruction2": {
    "description": "Step 2 interaction prompt for Stage 5 in wide layout"
  },
  "stage5Instruction3": "Step 3 of 3: Click the sinogram border bars on the right to examine 1D cross sections along constant θ or r.",
  "@stage5Instruction3": {
    "description": "Step 3 interaction prompt for Stage 5 in wide layout"
  },
  "stage5NarrowDescription": "The bottom graph displays the 1D cross section of g(r, θ) along the probe trajectory. Summing these projections over all angles (backprojection) yields a large integral inside the phantom and a smaller integral outside, but unfiltered backprojection produces a blurry 1/|r| image.",
  "@stage5NarrowDescription": {
    "description": "Concise description text for Stage 5 in narrow layout"
  },
  "stage5NarrowInstruction1": "Step 1 of 3: Click inside the phantom disk on the top. Notice the large backprojection integral on the bottom graph.",
  "@stage5NarrowInstruction1": {
    "description": "Step 1 interaction prompt for Stage 5 in narrow layout"
  },
  "stage5NarrowInstruction2": "Step 2 of 3: Click outside the phantom disk. Observe how profile values drop and the integral becomes small.",
  "@stage5NarrowInstruction2": {
    "description": "Step 2 interaction prompt for Stage 5 in narrow layout"
  },
  "stage5NarrowInstruction3": "Step 3 of 3: Click the sinogram border bars on the bottom to examine 1D cross sections along constant θ or r.",
  "@stage5NarrowInstruction3": {
    "description": "Step 3 interaction prompt for Stage 5 in narrow layout"
  },
  "stage5NarrowSubtitle": "1D Profiles & Backprojection",
  "@stage5NarrowSubtitle": {
    "description": "Subtitle for Stage 5 in narrow layout"
  },
  "stage5NarrowTitle": "Cross Section",
  "@stage5NarrowTitle": {
    "description": "Short title for Stage 5 in narrow layout"
  },
